\bigskip
\noindent
\textbf{Aspirational boundary, not a proven one.}
The framekernel segment of the matryoshka is the least validated layer
in the Telix design.  Unlike Asterinas, which brings \emph{all}
microkernel servers into a single Rust-enforced address space, Telix
only pulls in thin, performance-critical layers (page-table
manipulation, shootdown, the LLFree allocator) where the static
guarantees of Rust's type system plausibly substitute for hardware MMU
isolation.  The remaining servers—external pagers, personality
servers, filesystem servers, device drivers—run as conventional
isolated processes.

This hybrid boundary has not been attempted in any prior system: it
relies on Rust's safety promises holding in the hostile environment of
a kernel address space shared with untrusted (but MMU-isolated) code.
If the boundary proves unsound—if privilege escalation through the
Rust type system cannot be ruled out—the latency-motivated framekernel
components (the LLFree allocator and scheduler management loop) are
promoted to userspace servers, each behind an MMU boundary.  The
privileged set — page-table manipulation, shared-page-table
management, TLB shootdown, and IOMMU management — remains in the
kernel, exactly where it would be in seL4, L4, or any microkernel.
The rest of the architecture (page clustering, CPS state machines,
capability transports, external pagers, the verification stack) does
not depend on the allocator or scheduler being co-resident with the
privileged set; the system runs either way, just slower in the
degraded configuration.

The control plane is complicated by adaptations to cover these
matryoshka domains, where protection/isolation boundaries are
deliberately aligned with hardware-affinity boundaries.

\section{Protection/Affinity Dual-Purpose Boundaries}
\label{sec:protection_affinity}

Protection boundaries are placed so that they coincide with hardware
affinity boundaries.  A partition boundary between two NUMA nodes is
simultaneously an isolation boundary and the natural edge of a
coherence and message-passing domain, so the cost of enforcing
isolation (address-space switches, TLB shootdowns, message
serialisation) is paid exactly where the hardware imposes a
communications cost anyway.

\section{Per-\foreignlanguage{russian}{Матрëшка}/Matryoshka Layer IPC}

Each layer of the matryoshka has a different IPC mechanism suited to
its communication cost.  Register-based trapping IPC (not
blocking-synchronous like \Mach\ or
\seLFour) coexists with the FlexSC/\iouring\ model because the two
mechanisms serve different scopes: a shared-memory ring cannot survive
transmission over the wire (or airwaves) to a remote cluster node, so
the ring is used where memory is shared and register IPC where it is
not.  The four scopes and their mechanisms are summarised in
Table~\ref{tab:ipc_scopes}.

