\section{Within a Framekernel Domain: Continuation Calls}
\label{sec:within_framekernel}

Within a framekernel domain, state machines and continuations are the
fundamental control-flow dispatch mechanism: the interrupt model of
programming is used in lieu of the task model.  Rust's \texttt{async}
desugars this continuation-passing style into ordinary-looking
\texttt{await} code, so the burden of manually managing continuation
state is carried by the compiler rather than the programmer.
Microkernel minimality bounds the amount of code written in this
interrupt style, which matters because the style, while compact, is
more burdensome to write by hand than the task model.

\section{Kernel--User Boundary: FlexSC-Like Mapped Rings}
\label{sec:kernel_user_ring}
\index{FlexSC}\footcite{soar10}

Most things dealt with by conventional monolithic kernels via function
calls are instead done by ``userspace'' processes, which then use the
syscall API/ABI.  The scare quotes reflect that these processes are
highly privileged: they manipulate page tables \index{pagetable} and
execute privileged instructions (for instance, filling the
TLB\index{TLB}).  ``External pagers'' are the canonical example ---
``userspace'' processes that act in the place of an in-kernel virtual
memory API.

\section{Between Multikernel Domains: Zero-Copy IPC}
\label{sec:between_multikernel}

Because multikernel domains align with NUMA\index{NUMA} nodes (or small,
topologically nearby groups of them), a ``remote'' access penalty is
paid when crossing a domain boundary, but it is of vastly smaller
magnitude than the penalty for a remote cluster\index{cluster} node.

\section{Between Cluster Nodes: Network-Transparent IPC}
\label{sec:between_cluster_nodes}

While shared memory can be exploited for zero-copy transfer between
\multikernel\ domains (typically aligned with \NUMA\ nodes),
\cluster\ nodes are another matter entirely: there is no way to avoid
much slower I/O operations to transmit data between them.
